\textit{(Adapted from \cite{LWX126}, Cor. 1.5.)}
For $j=5$ or $j=6$, the class $h_j^2\in E_2^{2,2^{j+1}}$ survives nontrivially to $E_\infty$ and belongs to the list $L$ above.
\par\emph{Proof.} For $j=5$, use the fixed Literature permanence input. For $j=6$, use the main permanence theorem. In either case the class is the product of the two standard $h_j$ classes, transported along the bidegree equation, and $j\leq6$.

\textit{(Adapted from \cite{LWX126}, Cor. 1.5.)}
For every $x\in E_2^{2,128}$, nonzero survival to $E_\infty$ is equivalent to $x=h_6^2$.
\par\emph{Proof.} The fixed $E_2$ basis input says that $x=0$ or $x=h_6^2$. A zero $E_2$ image of a common permanent representative has zero $E_\infty$ image, so nonzero survival excludes the first case. Conversely, $h_6^2$ survives by the main theorem.

